Adapt node's Mach-O SEA injection to the LIEF 1.x Section API.

LIEF 1.0.0 made the MachO::Section constructors non-public and replaced them
with static factories. 0.17.6 had public Section(std::string name) and
Section(std::string name, content_t content) (MachO/Section.hpp:112-113); in
1.0.0 the default and copy constructors moved to `protected:`
(MachO/Section.hpp:352-356) and creation goes through:

  static std::unique_ptr<Section> create(std::string name,
                                         const content_t& content,
                                         TYPE type = TYPE::REGULAR);

Building node 25.8.2 against a shared LIEF 1.0.0 therefore fails twice:

  ../src/node_sea_bin.cc:184: error: no matching function for call to
    'LIEF::MachO::Section::Section(const std::string&,
                                   const std::vector<unsigned char>&)'
  ../src/node_sea_bin.cc:169: error: 'LIEF::MachO::Section::Section()'
    is protected within this context      (after a naive setter-based fix)

Rewritten to use the factory. `add_section` still takes `const Section&` on
both SegmentCommand (SegmentCommand.hpp:248) and Binary (Binary.hpp:438), so
the call sites just dereference the unique_ptr. Semantically identical.

Two call sites, both in InjectIntoMachO: the create-new-segment branch and the
existing-segment branch.

WHY THIS MATTERS ON LINUX: node_sea_bin.cc compiles the ELF, Mach-O and PE
injectors unconditionally — the format is chosen at RUNTIME
(`if (LIEF::ELF::is_elf(exe)) … else if (LIEF::MachO::is_macho(exe)) …`), and
the only #ifdefs in the file are HAVE_LIEF and DEBUG. So a Mach-O API break
fails a Linux build. That is deliberate upstream: node's SEA can cross-target,
so compiling the Mach-O path out would remove a real capability rather than
dead weight.

Node upstream has not migrated — node main still vendors LIEF 0.17.0 — so this
is a local divergence until it does. Drop it when node moves to LIEF 1.x.

Diff generated with `git diff` against the v25.8.2 file rather than
hand-written, so the context lines are well-formed.

